sentences.l:
And himself he sat down opposite divine Odysseus
by the other wall, and to the gods to sacrifice he bade
Patroclus, his own companion;

but not of a lovely feast are the doings a care,
but, a very great woe, Zeus-nurtured one, beholding,
we fear;

and Hector, greatly in his strength exulting,
rages terribly, trusting in Zeus, nor at all does he honour
men nor gods;

for he claims that from the ships he will cut off the topmost stern-ornaments,
and the ships themselves burn up with devouring fire, and the Achaeans
cut down beside them, stirred up by the smoke.

‘My child, strength Athena and Hera
will give, if they wish, but you, your great-spirited heart
to hold in your breast; for kindliness is better;
